    'Hearing equipment' => [
        'cat' => 'Assistive technology & equipment',
        'short' => 'Hearing equipment helps people who are deaf or hard of hearing, and includes hearing aids, alerting devices and other assistive listening technology.',
        'overview' => "Hearing technology ranges from hearing aids worn in or behind the ear to devices that amplify a television or phone, and alerting systems that use flashing lights or vibrations to signal a doorbell, alarm or baby monitor. Some people use cochlear implants, which are a different kind of technology that a specialist team assesses and provides.\n\nAn audiologist tests your hearing, explains the results and recommends devices that suit your hearing loss, lifestyle and budget. Fitting is a process: devices are programmed to your hearing and adjusted over time as you get used to them and as your needs change.\n\nGetting used to hearing devices takes patience. Wearing them regularly, going back for adjustments and learning how to clean and care for them all help. Ask about follow-up appointments and support, and whether family and workplaces can be shown how to communicate in ways that help you.",
